const READING_SETS = [
{title:'Cooling crowded cities',target:0,text:`As summers become more <b>prevalent（広く見られる）</b>, cities are reconsidering how streets and homes are designed. Dark roofs and wide roads can <b>accumulate（蓄積する）</b> heat, while a lack of trees makes outdoor travel difficult. Planting trees can <b>mitigate（和らげる）</b> the problem, but it is not a complete answer. Young trees need water and maintenance, and land is often a major <b>constraint（制約）</b>. The most successful plans use local temperature data to identify neighbourhoods where residents face the greatest risk.`,main:'Urban cooling needs local, practical planning rather than one simple solution.',claim:'Planting trees removes the need for any other cooling policy.',tf:'FALSE',inference:'Cities should target resources at neighbourhoods with the greatest need.',purpose:'to explain why urban cooling requires more than tree planting'},
{title:'The hidden cost of food waste',target:23,text:`Food waste is often discussed as a household problem, yet supermarkets and farms also lose large quantities before food reaches consumers. Much of this loss is linked to strict appearance standards for fresh produce. Although retailers may <b>justify（正当化する）</b> these standards as a way to protect quality, imperfect-looking fruit is frequently edible. Some businesses now sell it at lower prices, which can <b>generate（生み出す）</b> income while reducing waste. The approach is not universally <b>feasible（実行可能な）</b>, but it challenges the <b>conventional（従来の）</b> idea that appearance always indicates quality.`,main:'Changing retail standards can reduce food waste without reducing food quality.',claim:'All supermarkets have already stopped using appearance standards.',tf:'NOT GIVEN',inference:'Selling imperfect produce may benefit both retailers and consumers.',purpose:'to challenge an assumption about food quality'},
{title:'Why public transport projects fail',target:43,text:`New rail lines are often announced as symbols of progress, but building them is only one part of the challenge. A system needs reliable <b>infrastructure（インフラ）</b>, clear information and links to other forms of transport. When these elements are missing, passengers may continue to use cars. Researchers <b>assess（評価する）</b> a project not only by passenger numbers but also by whether it changes daily travel behaviour. A short-term rise in use may be <b>negligible（ごくわずかな）</b> if the service later becomes unreliable.`,main:'Transport projects must be evaluated by lasting changes in travel behaviour.',claim:'Passenger numbers are the only useful way to assess a rail project.',tf:'FALSE',inference:'Good connections and reliability affect whether people change transport habits.',purpose:'to explain a broader measure of transport success'},
{title:'The value of urban wetlands',target:35,text:`Urban wetlands were once treated as unused land, but many cities now recognise their value. They can store water after heavy rain, provide habitats and improve local air quality. These benefits are <b>empirical（実証的な）</b>: researchers have measured lower flood damage near restored wetlands. However, the <b>duration（期間）</b> of restoration matters. Ecological systems do not recover immediately, so early results may be ambiguous. A coherent plan therefore monitors changes over many years rather than expecting instant improvements.`,main:'Wetland restoration brings several benefits, but its effects need long-term monitoring.',claim:'Wetlands recover fully as soon as restoration begins.',tf:'FALSE',inference:'Early evidence alone may not show the final value of a restored wetland.',purpose:'to show why patience is needed in environmental restoration'},
{title:'Remote work and regional inequality',target:8,text:`Remote work has allowed some employees to live farther from major business centres. Supporters argue that it can reduce a <b>disparity（格差）</b> between expensive cities and smaller towns. Yet the benefit depends on digital access. In areas with weak broadband, remote work remains difficult, regardless of how <b>abundant（豊富な）</b> jobs may appear online. Governments that <b>allocate（割り当てる）</b> funds to faster connections may enhance opportunities, but technology alone cannot eliminate differences in education or local services.`,main:'Remote work may reduce regional inequality only when digital access and other conditions improve.',claim:'Strong internet connections completely eliminate regional inequality.',tf:'FALSE',inference:'Digital infrastructure is necessary but not sufficient for equal opportunity.',purpose:'to qualify an optimistic claim about remote work'},
{title:'Measuring scientific claims',target:42,text:`Scientific findings often appear certain in news reports, yet researchers normally begin with a <b>hypothesis（仮説）</b>, not a final answer. They collect evidence, test alternative explanations and modify their methods when results are unclear. A single study may <b>exhibit（示す）</b> an interesting pattern, but that pattern must be repeated before it becomes compelling. This process does not make science weak; it is a fundamental feature of careful inquiry.`,main:'Scientific knowledge becomes stronger through repeated testing and revision.',claim:'A single interesting study is enough to prove a scientific claim.',tf:'FALSE',inference:'Uncertainty can be a normal part of reliable scientific work.',purpose:'to explain how scientific confidence is built'},
{title:'The economics of repair',target:46,text:`For decades, many manufacturers encouraged consumers to replace broken products rather than repair them. This model can appear efficient, but it also creates waste. Repair programmes seek to <b>extend</b> a product’s useful life by making parts and instructions available. Where repair is difficult, consumers cannot easily <b>modify（修正する）</b> or restore products themselves. Some governments now intervene by requiring companies to provide spare parts. Critics worry about cost, but supporters argue that longer product lives can justify the initial investment.`,main:'Repair policies aim to reduce waste by making products easier to maintain.',claim:'Supporters believe repair policies may be worth their initial cost.',tf:'TRUE',inference:'Access to parts and instructions affects whether consumers can repair products.',purpose:'to describe a policy response to product waste'},
{title:'Tourism after a crisis',target:31,text:`When international travel declined, many regions turned to <b>domestic（国内の）</b> visitors. This shift helped some small businesses survive, but it also revealed a <b>fundamental（根本的な）</b> problem: popular destinations can become crowded even when overseas visitors are absent. Visitor numbers fluctuate with school holidays, weather and transport prices. Local authorities now monitor these patterns and encourage travel outside peak periods, rather than simply trying to attain the highest possible total number of visitors.`,main:'Tourism planning should manage visitor patterns, not simply maximise visitor numbers.',claim:'Domestic tourism always prevents overcrowding.',tf:'FALSE',inference:'A lower total number of tourists does not necessarily mean a destination is less crowded.',purpose:'to question a simple measure of tourism success'},
{title:'The language of public warnings',target:16,text:`Public warnings must be clear enough for people to act quickly. An <b>ambiguous（曖昧な）</b> message can lead different groups to make different choices, especially during emergencies. Officials therefore try to use explicit instructions and avoid technical terms unless they are explained. Yet warning systems also need to be flexible: a message suitable for one event may not be equivalent to a message needed for another. The goal is not merely to send information, but to help people understand what they should do next.`,main:'Effective public warnings need clear language that fits the specific situation.',claim:'Technical terms should always be used in public warnings.',tf:'FALSE',inference:'The same warning style may not work for every emergency.',purpose:'to explain the purpose of clear risk communication'},
{title:'When prices move together',target:22,text:`Prices for energy, food and transport sometimes rise at the same time. These <b>concurrent（同時の）</b> changes can place pressure on households, particularly when wages do not increase. Economists try to isolate the causes because events that coincide do not always have the same cause. For example, a drought may affect food prices while a separate political event affects energy supply. Understanding these discrete influences helps governments design policies that address the right problem rather than treating every price rise as identical.`,main:'Policymakers need to separate different causes when several prices rise together.',claim:'Events that happen at the same time must share the same cause.',tf:'FALSE',inference:'A policy based on the wrong cause is less likely to solve a price problem.',purpose:'to warn against confusing coincidence with causation'}
];
